  "worked_solution": "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{\u2032}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(\\frac{7}{2})=\\frac{{f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{7}{2})\\cdot g(\\frac{7}{2})-f(\\frac{7}{2})\\cdot {g}^{\u2032}(\\frac{7}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{7}{2}))}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(\\frac{7}{2})=g(\\frac{7}{2})=1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (3,4)$ equals $-2.$ So, ${f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{7}{2})=-2.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $0.$ So, ${g}^{\u2032}(\\frac{7}{2})=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\frac{{f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{7}{2})g(\\frac{7}{2})-f(\\frac{7}{2}){g}^{\u2032}(\\frac{7}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{7}{2}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (1)-1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
